Melasma
Dermatology & Soft Tissue
Symmetrical facial hyperpigmentation, Sun-exposure exacerbation, Pregnancy/OCP trigger

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DST-029
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe psychosocial distress and panic attacks secondary to sudden noticeable darkening of facial pigmentation, fearing a rapidly spreading skin cancer.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, Partner
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a young patient experiencing extreme anxiety and hyperventilation, fearing they had developed a rapidly spreading "black skin cancer" across their face after spending the weekend outdoors. Urgent assessment was required to definitively diagnose the benign nature of the hyperpigmentation, rule out acute systemic pathology (e.g., Addison's crisis), and provide immediate psychological de-escalation before the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My face has turned completely dark brown over the last two days. It looks like a giant, spreading skin cancer across both my cheeks and forehead. I'm terrified."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports noticing faint, symmetrical hyperpigmented patches on their cheeks over the last few months. However, following a full weekend of outdoor ultraviolet light exposure (beach trip), the patient experienced a sudden, dramatic exacerbation and darkening of the lesions over the last 48 hours. The rash presents as symmetrical, irregular, poorly defined light to dark brown patches. The predominant distribution is strictly on the sun-exposed areas of the face including the cheeks, forehead, and upper lip. The patient notes a complete absence of scaling, textural changes, pruritus, or pain. The patient recently started a new oral contraceptive pill 3 months ago. The rapid darkening triggered acute health anxiety.
Functional Status: Independent, currently experiencing high acute anxiety.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe health anxiety regarding malignancy.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 128/82 | HR: 95 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 64 Kg
General: Tearful, highly anxious, frequently looking in a hand mirror.
Physical Exam: The face demonstrates prominent, symmetrical hyperpigmented macules and patches across the bilateral malar cheeks, the central forehead, and the cutaneous upper lip. The color is a uniform medium-to-dark brown. Crucially, the skin texture is entirely normal; there is a complete absence of palpable induration, scaling, epidermal atrophy, or ulceration. The borders of the pigmentation are irregular and poorly defined, blending smoothly into normal skin. There are no signs of systemic hyperpigmentation (e.g., palmar creases, oral mucosa are normal).
Environmental Observations: Clean environment.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient commenced a combined oral contraceptive pill 3 months ago.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Melasma (Acute UV-induced exacerbation) - Status: Acute Exacerbation
Differentials:

Lentigo Maligna / Melanoma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The completely symmetrical, bilateral distribution, the lack of any palpable textural changes, and the smooth, macular nature of the pigmentation.

Addison's Disease (Primary Adrenal Insufficiency) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal blood pressure, absence of systemic weakness/vomiting, and lack of pigmentation in the palmar creases or oral mucosa.

Post-inflammatory Hyperpigmentation - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of a preceding inflammatory skin condition (like severe acne or eczema) in the exact areas of the current pigmentation.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low (benign condition, high anxiety)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents in a state of acute panic driven by a rapid, UV-induced exacerbation of classical Melasma. Triage correctly recognized the severe psychological distress and fear of malignancy as an acute crisis. Immediate out-of-hours clinical assessment was vital to physically examine the skin, definitively rule out any atypical or malignant features, and provide immediate, authoritative clinical reassurance. By diagnosing this as a common, benign, hormonally-driven condition (exacerbated by their recent OCP and sun exposure), the intervention immediately resolved the acute psychological crisis and avoided an unnecessary and highly anxious presentation to an emergency department.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Provided extensive clinical reassurance that the lesions are entirely benign and represent zero risk of skin cancer. Discussed the direct correlation between the oral contraceptive pill, the recent heavy sun exposure, and the rapid pigment darkening.
Positive Behavioral Change: By applying a thick layer of SPF 50+ broad-spectrum sunscreen every single morning, regardless of the weather, you will physically block the UV rays that signal your skin to produce this dark pigment, preventing the patches from worsening. By wearing a wide-brimmed hat whenever you are outdoors, you will provide the essential physical shade required to allow the dark pigment to slowly fade over time.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not required.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not required.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 2-4 weeks to discuss potentially ceasing or altering the oral contraceptive pill, and to discuss topical lightening therapies (e.g., hydroquinone) if desired.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If you develop new, raised, crusting spots within the pigmented areas, or if you begin feeling systemically unwell with severe dizziness or vomiting, present to a medical centre or Emergency Department.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Melasma is a benign but often chronic cosmetic condition. It is stubbornly resistant to quick treatments.
Clinical Course: The sudden darkness will slightly fade as the acute sun exposure passes, but the baseline pigmentation will remain as long as the hormonal triggers (OCP) and incidental UV exposure continue.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Routine skin checks with your GP are still recommended, but this specific symmetrical pattern is not dangerous.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The combination of the hormones in your new pill and the intense sun from the weekend caused the pigment cells in your face to go into overdrive, dumping extra brown melanin into your skin. It is entirely safe. Sun protection is the absolute most important treatment.
Actionable Steps: Start using daily SPF 50+ immediately. Do not use harsh physical scrubs or at-home chemical peels, as irritating the skin can actually make the pigmentation darker.

